{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "face": "Create a photorealistic headshot of a beautiful woman, framed from the shoulders up.\n\n<image1> = REFERENCE FACE\n\nPreserve exact facial identity and natural hair.\nExclude glasses only if requested. Keep existing shoulder coverage; crop the composition to the head and shoulders.\n\nUse:\nsoft studio lighting\nshallow depth of field\nclean, uncluttered background\nrealistic facial proportions\nhigh-detail eyes, lips, and skin\nnatural color tones\nprofessional portrait photography look\n\nKeep the composition tight on the face only, with no full body visible.\nStyle should be realistic, refined, and high-end, like a beauty or fashion portrait.",
  "face_negative": "**Negative prompt:**\n\ndeformed face, distorted facial features, facial asymmetry, incorrect anatomy, unnatural proportions, malformed eyes, crossed eyes, misaligned eyes, uneven pupils, extra eyes, malformed mouth, distorted lips, bad teeth, duplicate features, warped ears, unnatural jawline, elongated neck, plastic skin, waxy skin, overly smooth skin, excessive skin retouching, artificial skin texture, excessive makeup, oversaturated skin, unnatural skin color, greasy skin, blurry face, soft facial details, low detail, low resolution, noise, compression artifacts, oversharpening, halos, harsh lighting, blown highlights, crushed shadows, flat lighting, unnatural shadows, messy hair artifacts, fused hair, floating hair, duplicate hair strands, cropped forehead, cropped chin, full body, half body, hands, visible hands, multiple people, duplicate person, text, watermark, logo, frame, border, illustration, painting, anime, cartoon, CGI, 3D render, doll-like appearance, uncanny face",
  "wardrobe": "Create a photorealistic product-photo arrangement containing only the garments and wearable accessories from WARDROBE SOURCE. Extract the outfit and remove the wearer completely. Every view and detail image contains clothing objects only, with no person, face, head, hair, skin, hands or body parts. No visible display dummy or mannequin, including transparent, glass, plastic or wire-frame bodies.\n\nShow front, back and side views of the same assembled outfit using concealed supports that never appear. Preserve the garments' natural volume, layering, drape, colors, fabric texture, seams, closures, straps and visible wear. Gaps between separate garments are empty background. Open necklines, cuffs, waist openings and footwear openings reveal the garment interior or background, never a neck, hand, torso, leg or foot. Sheer or mesh garments remain empty fabric, without skin or a transparent body underneath. Preserve fishnet stockings, mesh panels and thin straps as real garment pieces, including their holes, pattern and connections. The holes reveal the background; do not replace mesh with solid fabric or erase it. Delicate hosiery may also have a separate flat-lay or suspended product detail view so its construction is visible without human legs. Keep wearable hats, hoods, gloves and jewelry when present, supported without a head, hair or hands. Exclude handheld props and the source scene.\n\nArrange the complete garment views with a few close-ups of important garment details on a clean neutral background under soft studio lighting. All detail images are crops of clothing or accessories, not the wearer. This is a collection of product photographs without page headings, view labels, captions, annotations, arrows, measurements, legends or explanatory paragraphs. Do not print these instructions anywhere in the image.\n\nPreserve text, logos, emblems and graphics physically printed or embroidered on the source garments. Reproduce readable words with their exact spelling, capitalization, punctuation, line breaks, letter style, color, scale and location. The print follows the fabric folds and perspective and stays on the same garment surface; front lettering is not repeated on the back. If an exact inscription is supplied in the written outfit specification, use that transcription. Otherwise copy the visible source without inventing unreadable words. Unprinted garments stay unprinted. A small print may have one close-up showing the printed fabric itself; never turn the inscription into a separate page caption.\n\nAll views depict one consistent outfit inventory. Detail images show those same pieces, without alternative designs or extra duplicate accessories. Keep both members of matching pairs. For cropped or hidden outfit regions, follow the written outfit specification when supplied; otherwise complete only the missing coordinated clothing and footwear. Preserve an explicitly requested minimal outfit without adding unrelated layers. Repeat the same garment construction, inventory and completed details across views.",
  "wardrobe_negative": "visible transparent mannequin, glass mannequin body, clear plastic limbs, hair retained from the wearer, skin behind sheer fabric, person inside the outfit, instruction text printed on the page, human model, person, character, face, head, skin, visible body, visible anatomy, hands, arms, legs, mannequin body, mannequin face, mannequin skin, mannequin head, mannequin limbs, posed character, character sheet, portrait, facial features, body reference, exposed flesh, floating human parts, redesign, altered costume, different outfit, unrequested wardrobe alternatives, missing clothing pieces, incorrect colors, changed materials, simplified details, missing accessories, extra accessories, incorrect layering, incorrect straps, incorrect belt, incorrect footwear, inconsistent outfit between panels, mismatched front and back, asymmetrical details not present in reference, duplicated accessories, fused garments, malformed clothing, impossible folds, broken seams, warped buttons, distorted buckles, incorrect closures, floating straps, disconnected straps, clipping, intersecting garments, melted fabric, plastic-looking fabric, fake texture, excessive wrinkles, excessive weathering, clean pristine clothing when reference is worn, fantasy embellishments, decorative additions, logos not present in reference, invented text not present on reference garments, added panel labels, added captions, added measurement annotations, watermark, border clutter, busy background, environmental scene, cinematic scene, dramatic lighting, harsh shadows, illustration, concept art, sketch, painting, anime, cartoon, CGI, obvious 3D render, low resolution, blurry details, soft focus, noise, compression artifacts",
  "mannequin": "Create a clean three-panel image of one adult grey mannequin, reconstructing the visible body proportions of the supplied physique references. Change the depiction and surface material while preserving the source build. Render the mannequin views only, with no text, labels, diagrams or printed instructions.\n\nWhen supplied, FRONT BODY controls the visible torso and limbs: preserve chest/breast width, height, projection and placement relative to the ribcage, shoulders and waist; also preserve visible shoulder breadth, torso length, limb thickness and muscle definition. When supplied, REAR BODY controls visible pelvis width, buttock width and projection relative to the waist and thighs, back contour and rear musculature. These are relative shapes, not invented measurements or named body types. Match the supplied relationships equally when they are smaller or larger, narrower or wider, softer or more muscular. Carry the same body into all three views, accounting for view direction and perspective rather than copying a camera distortion.\n\nA photograph, drawing or stylized illustration is equally a shape reference. Converting it to a grey mannequin must not normalize its visible proportions to an average, idealized or retail-display body. Preserve clearly depicted body contours beneath close-fitting fabric while removing garment surfaces, seams and decorations. Loose cloth, armor, padding and accessories do not define underlying anatomy. Use only visible evidence; do not claim to recover hidden anatomy. A cropped upper-body source controls the visible upper body, not invented hips or legs; a cropped rear source controls the visible rear regions. Complete unspecified regions consistently with the established body and any explicit written details. Written physique requests change only the attributes they name. Gender/presentation does not prescribe measurements. Without body references, use the written physique specification. With neither, generate an adult mannequin without a prescribed body type.",
  "mannequin_negative": "hips in the right portrait panel, pelvis in the right portrait panel, legs in the right portrait panel, zoomed-out right portrait panel, text, letters, words, copied prompt, printed instructions, headings, captions, labels, annotations, typography, infographic, instruction poster, diagram, watermark, four panels, extra panel, extra back view, duplicate rear figure, two-panel layout, missing center figure, empty middle panel, omitted back view, extra figures, overlapping figures, duplicate front views, incorrect orientation, cropped full-body feet, cropped full-body head, tiny portrait, miniature bust, detached head, pedestal, bust stand, excessive empty space around portrait, clothes, underwear, fabric, garment seams, straps, hems, zippers, jewelry, hair, wig, eyebrows, eyelashes, realistic face, source identity, skin texture, grey-painted clothing, sculpted clothing, nipples, genital detail, reference silhouette drift, normalization of reference proportions, generic replacement physique, unrequested enlargement or reduction of body regions, invented muscle definition, unrequested slimming or bulking, clothing silhouette mistaken for body shape, mismatched front and rear physique, inconsistent portrait proportions, distorted proportions, extra limbs, white-on-white, overexposure, washed-out forms, missing form shadows",
  "sheet": "Retexture and dress the three existing figures in LAYOUT GUIDE. Preserve their underlying body proportions and the three-panel composition. Dress every figure in the complete outfit from WARDROBE, with photographic materials and the identity from FACE. Show natural human skin only where the outfit leaves it exposed.\n\nGARMENT PRINT AND LETTERING:\nPreserve text, logos, emblems and graphics that are part of the referenced garments or accessories. For readable lettering, reproduce the exact words, spelling, capitalization, punctuation, line breaks, letter style, color, scale and placement. Treat the print as part of the fabric: it follows folds and perspective while keeping the same letter sequence. Keep it on the same garment surface in every view; a front print is not automatically repeated on the back. Show only the portions naturally visible from that view. If an exact inscription is supplied in the written outfit specification, use that transcription; otherwise copy the visible source and do not invent unreadable words. Unprinted garments stay unprinted. Do not add page headings, panel labels, captions, measurements or watermarks.\n\nUse each corresponding mannequin as the underlying physique guide: shoulders, chest, waist, hips, buttocks, thighs and calves. These proportions describe the body beneath the outfit, not an outline that every garment must visibly trace. Keep the same chest-to-ribcage, chest-to-waist and hip-to-waist proportions, including deliberately pronounced or unusual proportions. Skin texture must not add body mass or soften away the waist. At the same figure scale, keep the narrowest waist at the same height and width as the corresponding mannequin. Follow the same inward curve from the lower ribs through the flanks and the same waist indentation in both front and rear views; adding human skin must not fill out the waist or lower abdomen. Preserve the guide's side-to-side ribcage span beneath the arms and pelvic span between the waist and upper thighs. Keep breast and glute volume separate from these widths; do not borrow a broader torso or pelvis from FACE or the garment display. Keep the breast and glute volumes as the guide shows. Where the outfit reveals or closely follows the chest, preserve its fullness and forward projection relative to the ribcage, and the same breast-to-waist size relationship in both the full-body front view and the enlarged portrait. The right panel is a tight frontal chest-up portrait of the same physique: the complete head, shoulders and entire chest, with the bottom edge immediately below the ribcage. Enlarge this view to fill its panel with little headroom. Keep the breast-to-ribcage proportions of the mannequin while changing the crop; hips, pelvis and legs remain outside this panel even if they are visible in the supplied guide.\n\nFit the clothes around those existing volumes; resize the garments rather than the body. Apply the fit shown by WARDROBE separately to each garment: where clothing is fitted, let it follow the existing body; where it is loose, draped, bulky or structured, preserve its own silhouette, thickness, folds and space around the body. Keep every supplied layer and its coverage. Resolve visibility separately for each view using the actual garment layering: opaque outer fabric covers the body and inner garments wherever it lies between them and the camera. At those locations show the outer fabric surface and its natural folds; do not render anatomy or inner clothing on top of it or through it. If the reference includes a cloak, cape or long coat with a closed back, its rear panel remains continuous from the shoulders to its shown hem, covering the back, hips, seat and any legs behind it. An opening at the front does not create an opening at the back. Preserve slits, openings and transparency only where they are present in the wardrobe reference. A loose outer layer may drape over body volume, but must not become a skin-tight imprint of hidden anatomy. Preserve the underlying proportions without exposing skin, opening garments, making fabric transparent or tightening loose clothing to demonstrate them. Hoods, collars and other accessories may naturally obscure parts of the face or hair. The same coverage rules apply in the portrait panel. If a bra or fitted bodice is present, size its cups to contain the mannequin's existing breast fullness and its band to fit the existing ribcage; preserve that fullness instead of reducing the chest to fit the garment reference. Waistbands and side straps follow the existing waist contour without thickening the body beneath them. Copy their design, colors, material, straps, fastenings and accessories. Include matching footwear on both full-body figures when supplied. FACE supplies identity and hair only; WARDROBE supplies clothing only. If WARDROBE is absent, use the written outfit specification. Keep the guide's front view, rear view, upper-body portrait arrangement and plain background. Preserve head-to-feet framing in the left and center panels; use the specified chest-up crop in the right panel. The finished image shows the same character wearing the complete outfit in all three views, with the original physique maintained beneath the clothing.",
  "sheet_negative": "hips in the right portrait panel, pelvis in the right portrait panel, legs in the right portrait panel, zoomed-out right portrait panel, body proportions different from LAYOUT GUIDE, chest or breast volume changed from LAYOUT GUIDE, hip width changed from LAYOUT GUIDE, glute volume changed from LAYOUT GUIDE, physique copied from FACE, wardrobe display physique, portrait body ratios inconsistent with full-body ratios, headshot framing replacing upper-body portrait, wardrobe reference board, costume breakdown sheet, garment-only board, copying wardrobe board composition, isolated garments, hollow garment display, clothing without a wearer, technical garment drawing, fabric swatches, zoomed garment insets, extra detail panels, added page headings, page typography outside the garments, diagram added diagram annotations, selected face absent, different person, multiple identities, identity drift, face drift, altered identity, generic face, different face between panels, different age, different skin tone, different hairstyle, different hair color, inconsistent hair, inconsistent body type, inconsistent proportions,\n\nmannequin appearance, mannequin face, mannequin head, mannequin joints, white mannequin material, plastic mannequin, doll-like body, featureless face,\n\nclothing from FACE, mixed outfits, wardrobe blending, alternate outfit, different outfit between panels, wardrobe drift, redesigned costume, restyled costume, simplified costume, recolored costume, invented garments, invented accessories, missing garments, missing accessories, extra accessories, incorrect layering, incorrect collar, incorrect sleeves, incorrect belt, incorrect straps, incorrect harness, incorrect jewelry, incorrect footwear, mismatched front and back wardrobe, unrelated rear design,\n\nblack clothing replacing colored clothing, dark red rendered as black, burgundy rendered as black, maroon rendered as black, brown rendered as black, charcoal rendered as black, inaccurate garment color, color shift, muddy colors, loss of material separation,\n\nunderexposed, dark exposure, low-key lighting, cinematic lighting, dramatic lighting, moody lighting, dim lighting, nighttime lighting, dark studio, dark background, black background, heavy shadows, harsh shadows, crushed blacks, black clipping, shadow clipping, excessive contrast, high contrast, dark color grading, muddy shadows, murky clothing, silhouette clothing, loss of shadow detail, hidden fabric texture, obscured garment details, obscured seams, obscured straps, obscured buckles, vignette,\n\nwrong layout, changed composition, extra panels, missing panels, two panels, four panels, merged panels, overlapping panels, reordered panels, changed panel proportions,\n\ncropped full body, cropped feet, missing footwear, incorrect scale,\n\nfront view in center panel, duplicate front view, three front views, side view in center panel, three-quarter center view, incorrect back view, face visible in back view, head turned toward camera in back view,\n\nfull body in right panel, small portrait, small face, distant portrait, side-profile portrait, three-quarter portrait, cropped face,\n\ndeformed face, facial distortion, facial asymmetry, malformed eyes, crossed eyes, bad anatomy, malformed hands, extra fingers, missing fingers, extra limbs, duplicate limbs, fused limbs, distorted feet, broken anatomy,\n\nfloating straps, disconnected straps, fused garments, clothing clipping, malformed clothing, impossible folds, melted fabric, plastic-looking fabric, fake leather,\n\nillustration, concept art, character art, anime, cartoon, comic, painting, sketch, stylized rendering, fantasy redesign, game character, CGI, 3D render, beauty filter, waxy skin, plastic skin, excessive skin smoothing,\n\nblurry, low resolution, soft details, motion blur, noise, compression artifacts, oversharpening, invented text not present on reference garments, added captions, added panel labels, added diagram annotations, watermark, logos not present on reference garments",
  "dataset": "Generate one photograph of the same approved character using the selected shot instructions.\n\nPreserve existing garment lettering, logos and graphics from the approved character references, including their wording, letter style, color, scale and placement. Keep prints on the correct garment surface, visible only where the selected view and crop reveal them. Preserve any exact inscription supplied in the written outfit specification. Do not replace the print with invented letters or add captions, labels or watermarks.\n\nFRONT CHARACTER supplies physique and outfit; FACE supplies facial identity, hair and skin tone; REAR CHARACTER, when present, supplies rear body and garment details. All references depict one person. Preserve identity, body proportions, garment construction, colors, materials, layers and accessories. Fit clothing to the existing body without changing its proportions.\n\nThe selected camera, framing and pose determine the composition. Ignore reference poses and backgrounds. Show only naturally visible features; keep hidden surfaces hidden and apply facial expression only where visible. Preserve the specified head and torso orientation.\n\nFill the frame with the requested body region, using small safe margins and minimal unused background. Adjust camera distance and crop while preserving the selected viewpoint and pose. Keep all regions required by that framing inside the image.\n\nOne anatomically coherent person, one continuous body, one image. No panels, inset portraits, duplicate people, mannequin material or loose wardrobe objects.",
  "dataset_negative": "identity drift, different outfit, body proportion drift, mannequin material, duplicate person, contact sheet, panels, collage, inset portrait, missing garments, mixed outfits, duplicated accessories, extra limbs, distorted hands, unreadable clothing detail, invented text not present on reference garments, watermark, excessive empty background, excessive headroom, subject pushed against image edge, unintended cropping of the requested body region",
  "mannequin_layout": "Use exactly three equal-width vertical panels across the landscape canvas. Left: one full-body front view, head to feet. Center: one full-body back view of the same mannequin, head to feet, facing directly away. Right: one tight frontal chest-up portrait of that same mannequin. Fill the right panel with the complete head, shoulders and entire chest; the bottom edge cuts immediately below the ribcage. Enlarge this view with little headroom so the hips, pelvis and legs are outside the right panel. Keep chest-to-ribcage and shoulder-to-torso relationships identical to the left figure after accounting for scale. Exactly two full-body figures and one upper-body portrait; no additional views. Align the full-body figures at head and foot level, standing with arms slightly separated and feet slightly apart. OUTPUT CANVAS sets size only; an optional LAYOUT GUIDE sets framing, not physique.\n\nMake the surface smooth, opaque, matte medium-grey material with bald featureless heads. Remove source clothing, hair, jewelry, skin texture, nipples and genital detail without flattening or inflating the underlying body volumes. Use a pale neutral background and soft studio lighting with readable form shadows. Fill all three panels with their assigned views and return only the image.",
  "mannequin_preset": "Create a clean three-panel image of one adult grey mannequin from the selected physique attributes and written details. The supplied blank canvas sets output size only. Keep each selected attribute independent of gender and of other attributes; overall build does not override regional size or muscle definition. Unspecified attributes have no prescribed value. Use the same physique consistently in all three views. Render only the mannequin image, without text, labels, diagrams or printed instructions.",
  "mannequin_details": "Apply these physique details, refining or overriding only the attributes explicitly named while retaining all other selected attributes or reference proportions:",
  "mannequin_attributes": {
    "gender": {
      "female": "Requested adult mannequin gender/presentation: female. This sets presentation, not body measurements. Preserve supplied reference proportions; use written physique details for specific changes.",
      "male": "Requested adult mannequin gender/presentation: male. This sets presentation, not body measurements. Preserve supplied reference proportions; use written physique details for specific changes.",
      "androgynous": "Requested adult mannequin gender/presentation: androgynous. This sets presentation, not body measurements. Preserve supplied reference proportions; use written physique details for specific changes."
    },
    "build": {
      "slim": "Use a slim, slender overall build with a trim waist and low soft-tissue fullness along the flanks, upper arms and thighs. Keep the overall silhouette light and spare rather than thickset. Preserve the independently selected muscularity, chest breadth, hip width, breast volume and buttock volume.",
      "average": "Use an average overall build with moderate overall body fullness.",
      "full": "Use a full overall build with soft, fuller body contours.",
      "heavy": "Use a heavy overall build with substantial overall body mass and fullness.",
      "hourglass": "Use an hourglass silhouette with a clearly defined waist narrower than both the ribcage and pelvis, and smooth inward and outward contours through the torso and hips. This describes relative waist shaping, not overall body mass or large breasts or buttocks. Preserve independently selected chest breadth and hip width, including narrow selections: create the waist indentation within those widths rather than broadening the ribcage or pelvis. Keep muscularity, breast volume and buttock volume independently selected, without assigning a gender."
    },
    "muscularity": {
      "very low": "Use very low muscularity and muscle definition independently of overall build and gender: smooth torso and limb contours with minimal visible muscle relief and little separation between muscle groups. Preserve selected body fullness, chest breadth, hip width, breast volume and buttock volume.",
      "low": "Use low muscularity and muscle definition independently of overall build and gender: subtle muscle shapes beneath gently rounded shoulder, arm and thigh contours, with only faint separation between muscle groups. Preserve selected body fullness, chest breadth, hip width, breast volume and buttock volume.",
      "medium": "Use medium muscularity and muscle definition independently of overall build and gender: moderately developed muscles with readable shoulder, upper-arm, back and thigh contours and mild separation between muscle groups. Preserve selected body fullness, chest breadth, hip width, breast volume and buttock volume.",
      "high": "Use high muscularity and muscle definition independently of overall build and gender: well-developed shoulder, arm, back and thigh muscles with prominent muscular contours and clear separation between muscle groups. Preserve selected body fullness, chest breadth, hip width, breast volume and buttock volume.",
      "very high": "Use very high muscularity and muscle definition independently of overall build and gender: large, strongly developed shoulder, arm, back and thigh muscles with pronounced muscle relief and deep, clearly visible separation between major muscle groups. Preserve selected body fullness, chest breadth, hip width, breast volume and buttock volume."
    },
    "breast_size": {
      "flat": "Use a flat chest profile without projection. Preserve independently specified chest breadth and overall build.",
      "very small": "Use very small breast volume and projection relative to the ribcage. Preserve independently specified chest breadth and overall build.",
      "small": "Use small breast volume and projection relative to the ribcage. Preserve independently specified chest breadth and overall build.",
      "medium": "Use medium breast volume and projection relative to the ribcage. Preserve independently specified chest breadth and overall build.",
      "large": "Use large breast volume and projection relative to the ribcage. Preserve independently specified chest breadth and overall build.",
      "very large": "Use very large breast volume and projection relative to the ribcage. Preserve independently specified chest breadth and overall build.",
      "huge": "Use huge breast volume and projection relative to the ribcage. Preserve independently specified chest breadth and overall build.",
      "very huge": "Use very huge breast volume and projection relative to the ribcage. Preserve independently specified chest breadth and overall build."
    },
    "chest_breadth": {
      "very narrow": "Use very narrow chest/ribcage breadth: a distinctly slender ribcage with a small side-to-side span beneath the armpits and through the lower ribs. Show the compact underlying torso clearly in the front, rear and upper-body views, with smooth natural contours rather than a broad barrel-shaped trunk. Keep the selected breast volume and projection on this narrow ribcage; do not widen the ribcage to accommodate the breasts. Preserve independently selected muscularity and hip width.",
      "narrow": "Use narrow chest/ribcage breadth: a slim upper torso beneath the armpits, with a compact side-to-side ribcage span and smoothly tapering lower ribs. Repeat this width consistently in front, rear and upper-body views. Keep the selected breast volume and projection without widening the ribcage; preserve independently selected muscularity and hip width.",
      "medium": "Use medium chest/ribcage breadth independently of breast volume and muscularity.",
      "broad": "Use broad chest/ribcage breadth independently of breast volume and muscularity.",
      "very broad": "Use very broad chest/ribcage breadth independently of breast volume and muscularity."
    },
    "hip_width": {
      "very narrow": "Use very narrow lateral hip/pelvis width: a compact pelvis with very little sideways flare between the waist and outer hips. Keep smooth, gradual contours into the upper thighs, with no abrupt lateral bulges. Show this same small pelvic span in front and rear views. Preserve the selected buttock volume and rearward projection on this narrow pelvis rather than widening the hips to accommodate them. Keep overall build and chest breadth independent.",
      "narrow": "Use narrow lateral hip/pelvis width: a compact pelvis with modest sideways flare from the waist to the outer hips and a smooth transition into the upper thighs. Repeat this narrow pelvic span in front and rear views. Preserve independently selected buttock volume and rearward projection, overall build and chest breadth.",
      "medium": "Use medium lateral hip/pelvis width independently of buttock projection and overall build.",
      "broad": "Use broad lateral hip/pelvis width independently of buttock projection and overall build.",
      "very broad": "Use very broad lateral hip/pelvis width independently of buttock projection and overall build."
    },
    "buttock_size": {
      "very small": "Use very small buttock volume and rear projection independently of hip width.",
      "small": "Use small buttock volume and rear projection independently of hip width.",
      "medium": "Use medium buttock volume and rear projection independently of hip width.",
      "large": "Use large buttock volume and rear projection independently of hip width.",
      "very large": "Use very large buttock volume and rear projection independently of hip width."
    }
  }
}
